From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Elena Maher <12779416+elenaOnline@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 01:20:19 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] gpui: Add a hatch pattern background

A canvas drawing tldraw's `pattern` fill (short diagonal dashes in a
square tile anchored to the shape) had to build the dashes as geometry:
three round-capped strokes per tile, so a fill cost vertices in
proportion to its area times the zoom squared, a big rectangle millions.

hatch_pattern(tile, marks, to_pattern) is a Background (a new tag, like
PatternSlash and Checkerboard) that the fragment shaders draw: three
round-capped dashes along the descending diagonal of every unit tile of
pattern space, in `marks` over `tile`. `to_pattern` maps window pixels to
pattern space, so a pattern can be anchored, scaled, rotated or skewed
with the content it fills. The dashes form a lattice, so the nearest one
is found in constant time (two candidate rows); edges are anti-aliased
over a device pixel, and where the dashes get thinner than a pixel the
pattern fades to its average colour instead of aliasing. A path or quad
filled with it costs its own vertices whatever its size.

Background::hatch_filter(filter) widens the anti-aliasing footprint to
`filter` device pixels (at least one): how the pattern looks where it
stands for a bitmap tile that was resampled (tldraw's tiles, scaled by
the browser between power-of-two zooms). Coverage is a box of that
width across the nearest dash, so thin dashes lighten as they soften.

The transform is kept in the fields the other tags use for parameters;
Background::opacity leaves it alone, and paint_path and paint_quad scale
it to device pixels.

Metal, WGSL (validated by the gpui_wgpu naga tests) and HLSL
(unvalidated) shaders are updated alike.
